### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

This is related to https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11267 but presents a concrete example of where the spec diverging from implementations presents an actual behaviour difference.

The TLDR is that both Chromium and Firefox have observable behaviour different from that of the specification. I've found this while implementing this in WebKit and it would be good to agree whether the spec needs to change or whether the implementations should (WebKit is currently matching spec but can easily be updated).

```html
<dialog id="dialog" closedby="any"></dialog>
<script>
    dialog.show();
    dialog.oncancel = () => {
        console.log("cancel");
        dialog.removeAttribute('closedby');
    }
    dialog.requestClose();
    alert(dialog.open);
</script>
```

The above code should result in a closed dialog per the spec, but both Chromium and Firefox leave the dialog open.

When the [dialog.requestClose()](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#dialog-request-close) is called in the spec it sets a [flag](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#enable-close-watcher-for-requestclose()) on the dialog which forces [getEnabledState](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#the-dialog-element:create-close-watcher-getenabledstate) to return true (this flag is unset at the end of dialog.requestClose()).

Firefox and Chromium achieve this by setting a concrete enabled boolean on the close watcher, in lieu of the getEnabledState machinery.

The behaviour difference comes when you remove closedby within the oncancel. In Chromium (and presumably Firefox) there is an attribute changed step on dialog that looks at the [computed closedby state](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#computed-closed-by-state) and updates the dialog's close watcher's enabled flag. So in this case computed closedby is none, so the close watcher is disabled.

Then the requestClose continues and calls into Close Watcher close, which sees that the close watcher is disabled and early returns (leaving the dialog open).
